  "author": {
    "name": "Pablohorch"
  }
}

# Security Policy

## Supported versions

Security fixes are provided for the latest published version only.

## Reporting a vulnerability

Use [GitHub private vulnerability reporting](https://github.com/Pablohorch/opencode-goal-lite/security/advisories/new). Do not publish credentials, private objectives, local paths or exploitable reproduction details in a public issue.

## Security model

Goal Lite orchestrates OpenCode; it is not a sandbox. OpenCode, the selected provider, enabled tools, repository instructions and the local operating-system account remain separate trust boundaries. Autonomous continuation gives already-authorized tools more opportunities to run, so OpenCode permissions remain essential.

The plugin itself does not execute shell commands or read credentials. It reads session metadata, writes its own state directory and sends synthetic user messages through the OpenCode client.

Important controls include:

- fixed turn, active-time and effective-token ceilings;
- immediate pause for human intervention, rejected permissions and provider failures;
- a second state/message/idle check immediately before every continuation;
- one serialized event queue and one cancellable continuation per session;
- objective text escaped and labelled as user-provided task data, never injected as a system instruction;
- structured completion requiring a summary and at least one evidence item;
- owner-only POSIX modes, atomic replacement, per-session locks and symlink rejection;
- bounded lifecycle ledger and paused recovery after a process restart.

These controls reduce accidental loops and prompt confusion. They do not prove that evidence reported by an agent is true, encrypt local state or make untrusted repository content safe.

## Stored data

Persistence can contain the objective, blocker/recovery reasons, usage counters, agent/model identifiers and completion summary/evidence. It is stored outside the repository under the XDG state directory and is not intentionally uploaded by the plugin; the configured model provider still receives the objective through OpenCode.

Before sharing logs or state files, remove secrets, proprietary text, usernames, absolute paths and provider identifiers. File permissions are not encryption at rest.
